WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.790
From a katana sent in the mail&nbsp;
as a low-key threat to 100,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:10.380
hand drawn images, there's a whole lot you&nbsp;
probably don't know about "Princess Mononoke".

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.380
One of the most oft-repeated legends surrounding&nbsp;
"Princess Mononoke" concerns the authorship of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.360
the English translation. For years, it had&nbsp;
been rumored that famed British author Neil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.680
Gaiman wrote the English translation of the&nbsp;
script — but his name wasn't in the credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.480
so no one was really sure. Finally, in&nbsp;
2019, Gaiman took to Twitter to confirm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.100
that the rumor was indeed true. He claimed&nbsp;
that the reason his name wasn't there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.140
that when Studio Ghibli asked that some of the&nbsp;
Miramax executives be removed from the credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.320
they decided to remove Gaiman's&nbsp;
name instead of removing their own.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.540
Interestingly enough, Miramax had originally&nbsp;
asked Quentin Tarantino to rewrite the script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:54.000
When Tarantino turned the job down, he recommended&nbsp;
Gaiman instead. Gaiman's job would turn out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.580
more difficult than one might imagine, as he had&nbsp;
to deal with demands from several sides. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.540
there were Hayao Miyazaki's demands for&nbsp;
the script, then Miramax executives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.880
and finally, the specific demands of&nbsp;
notorious Miramax head Harvey Weinstein.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.460
As film producer Steve Alpert wrote in Polygon,&nbsp;
the bigwigs at Miramax thought American audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.080
wouldn't accept certain aspects of the film&nbsp;
without significant changes. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.820
one Miramax executive thought audiences wouldn't&nbsp;
believe Prince Ashitaka to be a prince at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.260
because he doesn't have a palace or fancy&nbsp;
clothes. However, Gaiman stuck to his guns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:33.900
and wound up producing what's often considered&nbsp;
one of the best translations of Miyazaki's work.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.360
Although Studio Ghibli has been partnering&nbsp;
with American studios like Disney and Miramax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.060
for many years now, these relationships haven't&nbsp;
always gone smoothly. For understandable reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.560
Hayao Miyazaki has often found himself butting&nbsp;
heads with studio executives who want to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.960
his films significantly to satisfy the American&nbsp;
market. In order to maintain the integrity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.280
"Princess Mononoke" as it went through the&nbsp;
translation process, Miyazaki came up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.680
a list of demands that he thought needed to be&nbsp;
included — or excluded — from the English script.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.240
According to Steve Alpert, Miyazaki's&nbsp;
suggestions were often about very specific ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:14.040
but he also dispensed advice about casting.&nbsp;
Alpert said that some of Miyazaki's comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:19.920
were things like "no modern slang" and "choose&nbsp;
good voices." He also wanted to make sure those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.360
involved in producing the dub were aware of the&nbsp;
cultural class distinctions present in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:29.160
such as the fact that Ashitaka is a prince&nbsp;
and thus behaves in an old-fashioned manner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:35.100
Miyazaki was also adamant that the firearms used&nbsp;
in the film never be called rifles — although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.383
that instruction may have been lost in&nbsp;
translation, judging by the final product.

00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:43.020
"This is the latest rifle that I've&nbsp;
asked these people to design. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.240
ones we've brought here have&nbsp;
turned out to be too heavy."

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.260
There's a famous urban legend&nbsp;
involving "Princess Mononoke"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.440
and a sword — and it happens to be mostly true.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.420
It's long been rumored that Hayao Miyazaki&nbsp;
sent a katana to Harvey Weinstein as a threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.800
while the American dub was in production.&nbsp;
The truth is that Miyazaki himself didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.540
send Weinstein a threatening message —&nbsp;
but his producer did. As the story goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.160
Weinstein was sent a katana in the mail, and&nbsp;
attached to the sword were two simple words:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.860
"No cuts." This was a serious worry,&nbsp;
as Weinstein was known in the industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.880
as "Harvey Scissorhands" because of how&nbsp;
much he liked to cut from his productions.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.760
While Miyazaki may not have&nbsp;
sent the threat himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.740
he did meet with the now-disgraced Weinstein&nbsp;
to hear his demands. He told The Guardian,

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.580
"I did go to New York to meet&nbsp;
this man, this Harvey Weinstein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:38.820
and I was bombarded with this aggressive attack,&nbsp;
all these demands for cuts. I defeated him."

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.480
Though we wouldn't recommend sending&nbsp;
swords through the post office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.540
it's certainly an effective way to&nbsp;
get a message across to your enemies.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:51.900
"Princess Mononoke" is an incredible feat of&nbsp;
animation, and considering its lengthy runtime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.220
it's no surprise the film took years&nbsp;
to complete. According to Miramax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.320
only about 10% of the film is computer&nbsp;
animated, which means the rest was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:07.740
drawn by hand. All in all, over 144,000&nbsp;
animation cels were created for the film.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:12.660
One of the most impressive scenes from an&nbsp;
animation standpoint comes near the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.340
of the movie. Ashitaka encounters a wild boar&nbsp;
that's become infected by a curse known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.080
Tatarigami, turning it into a terrifying&nbsp;
being covered in hundreds of snake-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.000
tendrils. One fan of the movie took to Twitter&nbsp;
to ask Studio Ghibli about the scene, writing —

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.740
"This has to be CG, right?"

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:34.740
It's a fair assumption, but it's totally wrong.&nbsp;
Studio Ghibli responded to the tweet, explaining —

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.060
"The part where the snakes move sluggishly&nbsp;
was so difficult that — even though the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.980
scene is only a couple of minutes long — it&nbsp;
took one year and seven months to finish!"

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.900
They noted that it apparently&nbsp;
took 5,300 drawings to complete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:53.940
and one of the Ghibli artists said it&nbsp;
got more and more confusing to draw as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.820
it went on. That painstaking effort definitely&nbsp;
helped make the scene as breathtaking as it is.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.460
The idea for the Tatarigami didn't come from&nbsp;
nowhere. In an interview with Roger Ebert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.640
Hayao Miyazaki described how&nbsp;
the boar monster was meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.840
represent the universal human&nbsp;
emotion of rage. He revealed —

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.220
"I'm a very emotional person, and&nbsp;
when I get enraged or furious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:23.400
I feel like black insects crawl out of my&nbsp;
pores. My staff are more peaceful, so it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:28.320
was difficult for them to imagine what it feels&nbsp;
like to be taken over by uncontrollable rage."

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.100
When Ebert asked if the creature&nbsp;
was based on his own personal rage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.840
Miyazaki conceded that it may be, but&nbsp;
he also explained that he thinks rage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:42.300
and violence are deep-rooted desires that all&nbsp;
human beings possess. The director continued —

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:47.580
"The issue we confront as human beings is&nbsp;
how to control and manage that impulse."

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.500
Though some might want to shield children&nbsp;
from these impulses, Miyazaki has maintained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.925
that it's important that they face this&nbsp;
reality, as it's a part of all our lives.

00:05:56.925 --> 00:06:02.400
"O nameless god of rage and&nbsp;
hate: I bow before you."

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.520
"Princess Mononoke" contains many diverse&nbsp;
characters and thematic elements, so it's only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:12.720
fitting that Miyazaki took inspiration from&nbsp;
numerous sources. According to the official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:18.120
"Princess Mononoke" website, Miyazaki is a huge&nbsp;
fan of the American director John Ford, who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:23.040
primarily known for making Westerns. Many of John&nbsp;
Ford's films — such as "My Darling Clementine" —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:27.840
take place in frontier towns, something Miyazaki&nbsp;
paid homage to with the settlement of Irontown.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.300
Irontown is filled with outcasts and outlaws&nbsp;
who have the strength to survive the tough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.200
environment of the frontier — much like&nbsp;
in your standard Western. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.340
"Princess Mononoke" also points out that&nbsp;
this strength and ambitious mastery over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:45.180
one's domain spells disaster and&nbsp;
destruction for the environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:48.960
which is exactly what Lady Eboshi's&nbsp;
hunt for the forest spirit represents.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:53.760
Compared to his previous movies like "Kiki's&nbsp;
Delivery Service" and "My Neighbor Totoro,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.740
"Princess Mononoke" is a strikingly dark and&nbsp;
serious film for Hayao Miyazaki. Speaking with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.540
Empire, the director described how risky the&nbsp;
project was, especially considering his prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:08.100
films. He explained that, while working on&nbsp;
"Porco Rosso," he learned of the war in former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:13.800
Yugoslavia, and was disappointed that humanity&nbsp;
kept repeating the same mistakes. He explained —

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.120
"After that, we couldn't go back and&nbsp;
make some film like 'Kiki's Delivery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.760
Service.' It felt like children&nbsp;
were being born to this world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:24.540
without being blessed. How could we&nbsp;
pretend to them that we're happy?"

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:29.340
Miyazaki has admitted that he exhausted the&nbsp;
animation team with the making of "Mononoke,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:33.180
which he expected would happen. But&nbsp;
even once the film was completed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:36.720
he wasn't sure about who the&nbsp;
audience was. He continued —

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.160
"At first I decided, 'This is something&nbsp;
children shouldn't see,' but in the end I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.880
realized, 'No, this is something that&nbsp;
children must see,' because adults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.340
they didn't get it — children understood it."

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:50.580
Once Miyazaki realized the&nbsp;
importance of the film he made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.800
he felt inspired to keep making movies again.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.140
"Life is suffering. It is&nbsp;
hard. The world is cursed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.086
but still, you find reasons to keep living."

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:07.020
In his list of requirements for the English dub of&nbsp;
the film, Hayao Miyazaki implored the executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:12.300
at Miramax to "choose good voices." Luckily,&nbsp;
casting directors heeded Miyazaki's advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:17.580
as the English-speaking voice cast for the&nbsp;
film is filled with sultry-voiced superstars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:23.520
Billy Crudup voices Ashitaka, Claire Danes&nbsp;
voices San, Minnie Driver voices Lady Eboshi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.080
Billy Bob Thornton voices Jiko-bô,&nbsp;
and Gillian Anderson voices Moro.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:33.060
Anderson was an especially big get for&nbsp;
the film, as she was at the height of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:38.280
"X-Files" fame when the English dub premiered&nbsp;
in 1999. At the film's premiere, Anderson was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:43.200
asked by Hollywood.com about her familiarity with&nbsp;
Miyazaki's films prior to starring in "Princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:48.180
Mononoke," to which she explained that she'd&nbsp;
been a fan of his movies for years, saying —

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:53.280
"His films are very spiritual and&nbsp;
very beautiful and very reverent."

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.600
As for whether she would show the film to her&nbsp;
children, Anderson wasn't sure at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.925
noting that "Princess Mononoke" was much&nbsp;
more intense than his previous movies.

00:09:02.387 --> 00:09:07.620
Lady Eboshi may be the most interesting&nbsp;
character in "Princess Mononoke," as she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.260
a very complicated woman who's neither all good&nbsp;
nor all bad. She's employed a group of former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.340
sex workers and given them important roles&nbsp;
in the continued functioning of Irontown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:22.020
and she provides a space for lepers who would&nbsp;
be turned away elsewhere. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.440
she's intent on furthering industrialization&nbsp;
at the expense of the environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.249
and she has no qualms about&nbsp;
killing the holy forest spirit.

00:09:29.249 --> 00:09:31.920
"When the forest has been&nbsp;
cleared and the wolves wiped out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.684
this desolate place will be&nbsp;
the richest land in the world."

00:09:34.684 --> 00:09:37.920
In a Twitter Q&amp;A with fans, Studio&nbsp;
Ghibli answered a question about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:40.710
character's backstory. The&nbsp;
folks at Ghibli revealed —

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:44.460
"Miyazaki has said that Eboshi was sold&nbsp;
as a slave and became the wife of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:48.060
leader of a band of Japanese pirates.&nbsp;
After she became a skilled fighter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:52.020
she killed him, stole his treasure,&nbsp;
and returned to mainland Japan."

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:56.940
This backstory explains how Lady Eboshi came&nbsp;
to be the confident, competent woman that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:02.400
she is — and why she has such a fondness for the&nbsp;
former sex workers employed in Irontown. Several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.380
commenters on Twitter noted what an interesting&nbsp;
character Lady Eboshi is, with one fan noting —

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:11.460
"She's exactly like a modern person.&nbsp;
She knows exactly what she has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:15.540
do to achieve her goals and has no&nbsp;
qualms about taking those actions."

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:20.700
Studio Ghibli fans are often keen to draw&nbsp;
connections between Hayao Miyazaki's many films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:26.220
and there's been a persistent rumor that one such&nbsp;
connection exists between "Princess Mononoke" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:31.020
his 1988 movie "My Neighbor Totoro." As the&nbsp;
story goes, the last remaining tree spirit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:35.820
at the end of "Princess Mononoke" goes on to&nbsp;
become one of the Totoros in the 1988 film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:40.380
Although "Totoro" came out before "Mononoke,"&nbsp;
it takes place several hundred years later.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:44.700
One fan asked Studio Ghibli whether&nbsp;
or not this rumor is true on Twitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:46.560
and the studio answered —

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:51.660
"During production, it seems that Miyazaki&nbsp;
said something along those lines to the staff."

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:57.180
While it may not be a categorical "yes," it does&nbsp;
seem like there's a good chance that Miyazaki&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:01.200
did make the connection between the two films&nbsp;
himself at one point. Either way, it's fun to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.880
think about all of Miyazaki's movies existing&nbsp;
in the same universe, or on the same timeline.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:11.220
While fans of "Princess Mononoke" might spend&nbsp;
much of their viewing time marveling at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.540
film's magnificent animation, there are&nbsp;
other elements of the film that deserve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:19.800
plenty of attention too. Sound design&nbsp;
is an important part of all filmmaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.180
and Studio Ghibli revealed a few fascinating&nbsp;
tidbits about this component of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:29.220
during their Twitter Q&amp;A. Ghibli revealed&nbsp;
that the distinctive noise the tree spirits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:33.720
make was created using wooden castanets,&nbsp;
as producers thought the spirits should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:37.440
have a wood-like quality about them&nbsp;
due to their connection with nature.

00:11:37.562 --> 00:11:42.523
[Clicking, rattling]

00:11:42.624 --> 00:11:47.160
Producer Toshio Suzuki revealed that one sound&nbsp;
design element he's proud of can be found during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:52.500
the fight between San and Lady Eboshi. According&nbsp;
to Suzuki, they spent a lot of time making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:57.780
Lady Eboshi's metal sword had a distinct sound&nbsp;
that was different from San's dagger, which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:03.420
made from bone. This makes for an excellent&nbsp;
scene with some incredibly dynamic audio.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:08.700
In many reviews of the film, critics have&nbsp;
praised "Princess Mononoke" for the way it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:13.200
doesn't include an overly simplistic happy&nbsp;
ending. In reference to the film's somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:18.060
ambiguous conclusion, an American journalist&nbsp;
asked Hayao Miyazaki if he thought of San&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:23.100
and Ashitaka as a tragic couple because they&nbsp;
can't be together. In response, Miyazaki said,

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:27.420
"They remain involved. I'm sure that&nbsp;
they maintain a good relationship."

00:12:27.420 --> 00:12:31.740
So it seems they aren't really the&nbsp;
tragic duo some perceive them to be.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:36.420
Even though they keep in touch, Miyazaki imagined&nbsp;
that the future will not be easy for either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:40.740
of them. He explained that Ashitaka will help&nbsp;
San stay alive, but that he will also dedicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:45.420
himself to helping the people of Iron Town —&nbsp;
and therein lies the difficulty. He continued —

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:50.880
"And this will tear Ashitaka apart, but this&nbsp;
won't stop him. Because he treasures both."

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:54.960
Miyazaki went on to say that this&nbsp;
difficulty is exactly the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.960 --> 00:13:00.180
lesson that contemporary viewers can take from&nbsp;
the story. It may not be a sappy happy ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:03.840
but according to the creator&nbsp;
himself, life indeed does go on.

